are you feeling uncomfortable insecure  scared anxious a fraud well if so then  you might very well be experiencing  impostor syndrome impostor syndrome  isn't simply a fear of failure it's a  fear of the inevitable moment that  people will find out that you don't know  what you're doing it's the constant  questioning about whether you belong and  the nagging feeling that you'll never  live up to the expectations that you  feel other people have for you I know  you might see things that I've done the  past interviews games successes and you  might feel that I have known exactly  what I was doing along that route but I  want to let you know that throughout all  of those times I was simply a girl  following a path just going with the  flow never really knowing what I was  doing I want you to know that I get so  nervous before I sing that I usually  don't talk to anyone for about an hour  beforehand because I'm too busy freaking  out inside my head yeah you'll be fine  babe and I get so anxious about  interviewing people or perhaps sometimes  just even talking to people in general  that I overanalyze everything that I've  said days or weeks after wondering did I  come across as stupid here will I be  remembered in a negative light and now  that I've graduated University and found  myself a job in the games industry which  is something that I've worked hard for  four years  I still find myself questioning whether  I should really be here and how long  it's going to last I basically want you  guys to know that I have impostor  syndrome or I did all right I sometimes  do but why am I saying all this to you  well I'm showing this with you because  the way that I'm getting over it is by  realizing that this feeling it's  universal if you feel like you don't  know what you're doing you are  definitely not alone in that ask  yourself if you enjoy what you're doing  not if you're good at it if you enjoy it  and ask yourself if you're comfortable  in the environment that perhaps you're  working in or you're living in and if  the answer to both of those is yes and  you still feel like an impostor then my  advice to you is to accept it we cannot  and should not know everything so my  personal interests lie in the creative  industry and it's been quite  mind-blowing to me to see how many  people have actually stood up and  accepted and shared that they to feel  like an imposter sometimes and they  don't know everything within the field  that they're working in it's interesting  to notice how many well-respected people  that are in the industry that actually  open up about the struggles that they  deal with and who's listening to those  people I realized that being open and  honest and authentic to your true self  Garner's a lot of respect  not because people are looking up to you  as someone who is better or more  knowledgeable but instead because these  people are able to make others feel like  they belong I'm just telling you off the  bat that I don't know anything and if  there's one thing that characterizes my  writing is that I always start from that  realization and I do what I can to keep  reminding myself of that during the  process I think we try to be experts  because we're scared we don't want to  feel foolish or worthless we want power  because power is a great disguise I even  feel odd calling myself a writer or a  screenwriter I do when I have to I put  it on my income tax form but I feel like  it's a lie even though it's technically  true if we live our lives striving to be  perfect and to know everything about our  chosen path then I honestly feel like  we've forgotten what it is that makes us  human  I mean a big part of the human  experience is knowing and understanding  that we are not going to understand  everything and that is what actually  makes life so exciting and it's what  makes us as humans so unique because  we're the ones with the intelligence and  the curiosity to actually try and find  those answers even though the answers  might not always be found curiosity is  what pushes humankind forever to find  out new solutions to our everyday  problems to create unique ideas that  nobody else has come up with before and  to push the boundaries of the knowledge  and industries that we as humans have  put in place because our collective we  still don't know it all and sure when we  try new things and embrace the unknown  there is a high chance that we are going  to fail  but there is no success without failure  so the sooner we become comfortable with  that fact and then the better will be  send stories out and and I pounded a  nail into the wall of my bedroom and  when I got the rejection slips back I  would put them on that nail and by the  time that I was 16 or 17 years old the  nail tore free from the plaster so I  just got a bigger nail and then when I  was 19 I sold a story to Starling  mystery stories it was called the glass  floor and they paid me like I think 35  bucks  that's my first sale so to summarize  this it's hard to be perfect but it's  even harder to accept the fact that  we're not but once you become  comfortable with that fact that's when  you allow yourself to become your  authentic self you don't have this  burden of needing to be something that  you can't bearing on your shoulders and  you can simply just carry on into the  unknown and enjoy whatever it is that  you want to do with your life so to stop  imposter syndrome we must understand  that we are not the best of what we do  we probably will never be the best of  what we do we won't know everything that  that ever is to know but if you're  trying and you're doing what you enjoy  and you're curious then that's all that  matters  you shouldn't have the pressure of  getting to the end and knowing it all to  hold you back  just enjoy the journey life is about  learning  not about knowing for have lovely day or  evening by  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

